course: chemistry
lesson: 13
title: Rust: iron's slow fire
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Leave a bike out in the rain all winter and its chain and bolts turn orange-brown and flaky. That orange stuff is rust, a brand-new substance, not dirt that has landed on the metal.
explain: Rust forms when iron joins up with oxygen from the air and with water. Take away either one, the water or the oxygen, and the iron hardly rusts at all.
explain: Rusting is a very slow cousin of burning: iron and oxygen join and give out a little heat. In a hand-warmer pack the iron is a fine powder, so it rusts fast enough for you to feel the warmth.
explain: Rust is flaky and falls away, so fresh iron underneath is uncovered and rusts next. That is why paint, a coat of zinc or a pinch of chromium mixed into the steel are used to keep air and water off.
explain: Other metals change too: copper slowly grows a green coat, like the Statue of Liberty, and that coat stays put and protects the copper underneath.
fact: Rust forms when iron or steel joins with oxygen and water; it is a new substance, so rusting is a chemical change.
fact: Iron in dry air, or in water with no air in it, hardly rusts; it needs both oxygen and water.
fact: In the classic three-nails test, the water is boiled to drive out air and oil is poured on top to stop air getting back in; only the nail in ordinary water with air rusts well.
fact: Salt from sea spray or from gritted winter roads makes iron rust faster, so cars and railings by the sea rust quickly.
fact: Rust is flaky and falls off, so it does not protect the iron underneath and rusting can go right through.
fact: Paint stops rust by keeping air and water away; once the paint is scratched, rust starts at the scratch and creeps under it.
fact: Galvanised steel is coated with zinc; the zinc reacts first and "sacrifices" itself, so the iron stays safe even at small scratches, and ships carry zinc blocks for the same reason.
fact: Stainless steel has chromium mixed in, which forms a thin invisible coat that heals itself; it can still get rust spots if left wet and salty for a long time.
fact: Rusting gives out heat; hand warmers use iron powder that rusts quickly once the pack is opened, while a rusty bike rusts too slowly to feel warm.
fact: The Statue of Liberty is made of copper sheets; it was shiny brown in 1886 and turned green as a protective coat called patina formed.
fact: Tetanus is caused by a germ in soil and dirt, not by rust; a deep cut from any dirty object can let it in, and vaccination protects you.
fact: Iron is found in rocks mostly joined with oxygen, as iron ore; people heat it with carbon in a furnace to pull the oxygen away and get iron.
fact: Mars looks red because its dust contains iron oxide, the same kind of substance as rust.
exercise: What two things does iron need from around it to rust? | Oxygen and water | Sunlight and heat | Salt and dry sand | Water and darkness
exercise: Why did the old bike left out in the rain turn orange-brown? | Its iron joined with oxygen and water to make rust | Orange road dust stuck to the wet metal | The rain washed the paint colour onto the metal | The metal was slowly melting in the cold rain
exercise: In the three-nails test, which nail rusts best? | The one in ordinary water with air | The one in dry air | The one in boiled water under oil | All three rust exactly the same
exercise: Why is oil poured on top of the boiled water in the three-nails test? | To stop air getting back into the water | To make the nail shiny and clean again | To feed the nail so it rusts faster | To keep the water from freezing
exercise: Why do railings by the sea rust faster than railings inland? | Salt from the spray speeds up rusting | Sea air has no oxygen in it at all | Sunlight at the seaside makes rust | Seagulls peck off the paint
exercise: Why does rust not protect the iron underneath it? | It is flaky and falls away, letting air reach new iron | It is so hard that it cracks the iron beneath | It soaks up sunlight and heats the iron | It is too thin to see so nobody notices it
exercise: How does paint protect an iron gate? | It keeps air and water away from the iron | It makes the iron too heavy to rust | It cools the iron down so it cannot react | It turns the iron into stainless steel
exercise: What is a galvanised bucket coated with? | Zinc | Pure gold | Chromium | Plastic
exercise: Why can a ship carry a block of zinc whose job is to dissolve? | The zinc reacts instead of the ship's steel | The zinc makes the ship float higher in the water | The zinc keeps barnacles and seaweed off the hull | The zinc is spare metal for repairs
exercise: What is mixed into stainless steel to stop it rusting? | Chromium | Shiny copper | Black carbon | A little lead
exercise: Why does a hand-warmer pack get hot when you open it? | Iron powder inside rusts fast and gives out heat | A tiny battery inside heats up a thin wire, like a toaster | Sunlight is stored in it from the factory | Your hand's heat bounces back off the pack
exercise: Why is the Statue of Liberty green? | Its copper grew a green patina over the years | It was painted green when it was built | Seaweed has grown all over its surface from the sea | Green lights shine on it every night
exercise: What really causes tetanus? | A germ from soil and dirt | The rust on old, dirty metal | The orange colour of the rust | Cold metal touching the skin
exercise: How do people get iron out of iron ore? | Heat it with carbon in a furnace | Wash it in lots of salty water | Leave it out in the sun to dry | Grind it into a very fine powder
exercise: Why is Mars called the Red Planet? | Its dust contains iron oxide, like rust | It is very hot like a glowing coal | It is covered in red sand from oceans | Red light from the Sun reflects only there
exercise: Is rusting a change you can easily undo? | No, rust is a new substance | Yes, just dry the metal out | Yes, rub it off and the iron returns | No, because the iron has melted
test: Max reckons rust is just orange dirt stuck on the bike, wash it and it's fine. What's true? | Rust is a new substance made from the iron itself | He's right, it is just road dust that sticks to the wet metal | It is old paint that has gone orange | It is a mould that grows on wet metal
test: You keep a nail in a sealed jar with a packet of drying crystals, which soak up any water. What will happen? | It will hardly rust, because there is no water | It will rust even faster than a nail left out in the rain | It will turn green like copper | It will rust only at night
test: "dad says if u scratch the car it'll rust there first. why tho?" | The scratch lets air and water reach bare iron | The scratch makes the metal much hotter there | Scratches pull salt out of the air like a magnet | The paint turns into rust at a scratch
test: Gran, washing her car after a snowy week: "it's the road salt". Why bother? | Salt left on the car speeds up rusting | Salt makes the paint change colour in the sun | Salt freezes hard and cracks the windows | Salt makes the car slower on the road
test: Ollie: "my old bike's all rusty so it must be warm, rusting makes heat". What's true? | It makes heat, but far too slowly to feel | Rusting takes in heat and slowly cools the bike down | He's right, rusty bikes feel warm | Rusting makes no heat at all
test: Mia says the green on the Statue of Liberty means it's falling apart and should be scrubbed off. What's true? | The green coat protects the copper under it | The green is rot that eats the copper away | The statue was always meant to be green paint | Scrubbing would make it green again faster
test: Leo stepped on a shiny, clean nail in a muddy field. "it's not rusty, so no worries". Right? | No, the germ comes from the dirt, so get it checked | Yes, only rusty nails are any risk | Yes, shiny metal kills any germs as soon as they touch it | No, shiny nails are more dangerous than rusty ones
test: If a cartoon robot made of plain iron went swimming in the sea every day, what would happen? | It would rust fast, sped up by the salt | It would turn green like a statue | It would stay shiny thanks to the salt | It would slowly turn into stainless steel
test: Which spoon would you expect to stay shiny for years in a wet kitchen? | A stainless-steel spoon | A rough plain iron spoon | A painted iron spoon, chipped | A bright copper teaspoon
test: "why'd they cover the fence in zinc, it's gonna corrode anyway innit". What's the point? | The zinc corrodes first and saves the steel | Zinc makes the fence stronger in high wind | Zinc is cheaper than painting it | Zinc keeps the snails off the fence
test: A space game says Mars is red because it is covered in lava. What's the real reason? | Its dust contains iron oxide, like rust | It is covered in red sea water | It is glowing red-hot all the time | It has red trees and plants
exam: Identify the two substances whose simultaneous presence is required for the rusting of iron. | Oxygen and water | Nitrogen and water | Oxygen and sea salt | Carbon and sunlight
exam: It is given that a steel article is coated with a layer of zinc. Select the statement most consistent with the established facts concerning such a coating. | The zinc reacts preferentially and thereby protects the steel | The zinc renders the steel incapable of conducting heat | The zinc converts the steel beneath it into stainless steel | The zinc accelerates the rusting of the steel
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of tetanus? | It is caused by a germ present in soil and dirt | It is caused by contact with iron oxide upon old metal | It is caused by the orange pigment of rust | It is caused by the coldness of metal objects
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which rusting, once commenced, proceeds into the depth of the metal. | The rust flakes away and exposes fresh iron | The rust conducts heat into the metal | The rust attracts magnets that tear the metal | The rust dissolves the paint on other surfaces
